Z||000000||mrgone||07-06-2001||12:10 AM||||well... i have a 2m4 and my oil pan is flaking really bad, i have a bit of an oil leak also, and was thinking that tthe next time i change my oil, i should change the pan.<P>is this a hard thing to do? i need a gasket and stuff dont i?<P>i mean, its flaking bad, wouldnt be very good if a large hole formed while i was cruising. especially with a non-working oil or temp guage.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_9.jpg">   <BR><B>86.5 2m4 5speed turd 241,000kms</B>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||07-06-2001||02:02 AM||||Try doing a search in the technical thread, in both new and old archieve.<P>I remember it being said on how to do it.<P>removal of alternator,starter, engine mount, <BR>and finally the oil pan,  very brief instructions.<P>I bought an engine hoist when i did mine, the safest way of doing it.  I had a new exhaust put on, and it did interfeared with putting the oil pan back on, making it a bit more difficult.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrgone||07-06-2001||11:59 AM||||ewww, sounds like a big job||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-06-2001||08:15 PM||||The engine mount is the big pain. You'll have to pull the AC to get one side. The other side is usually trapped under the lower alteranator bracket as I bremember. You need the alt loose but not off.<P>Of course you need to hold the engine up while you work.... That can be a pain. Even bigger pain if you don't hold it solid and it falls on you.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||07-07-2001||06:48 AM||||How are you wearing out an oil pan !!! lowering kit, low profile tires and driving country roads  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> if you drain the oil and clean the area real well, there are epoxies you can put over the bad spot. Id add some kind of metal skid plate if your gonna keep driving the same.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SteveJ||07-07-2001||08:01 AM||||He says its flaking from the outside with rust. See my thick layer of leaked oil forms a protective layer that keeps mine from rusting. . .||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||07-07-2001||07:19 PM||||GREAT......now I have a reason to leave mine all dirty and oily too. Pretty sure some epoxy over the rusty area would be fine if you get it really clean. Ive used it on metal gas tanks, lasted for years.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SteveJ||07-07-2001||08:55 PM||||Around here gas tanks rust out. I have gotten a year out of just a plain old fiberglass patch and that was on the seam. Would have lasted longer if it wasn't a seam. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||07-08-2001||11:22 AM||||When I bought my '85SE a few months back it the oil pan started leaking on one side from a pin hole through the flaked off rust.<P>  I cleaned the side real good, first with gas, then with rubbing alcohol, then I put on a patch of JB Weld epoxy and a piece of alum sheet metal, held in place with shipping tape while the epoxy cured overnight.<P>  If its flaking I would clean it up and take a screwdriver and peck at it to get all the loose stuff off, then put a big epoxy/sheet metal patch over the area where you think its thin.<P>  a few hours under the car now will save you a blown engine later, esp if your pressure gauge is not working.<P>EDIT: BTW i used some epoxy putty stuff from the autoparts store to patch up my leaky gas tank filler vent tube and foundout that stuff is porious - it still oozes gas through - not as bad as when it was leaking - but I would recommend you NOT use the putty epoxy stuff - get JB Weld.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-08-2001).]||||reg||10
